problems.solution:
**The point of this one is the rename, so run it for real** — the studio's Run button drives the library, not the runner, so the `--list` step needs your own terminal.

Create `week1/generated/day4-practice-broken.ts` (deliberately **no** `.spec.ts`):

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('login reaches the dashboard', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://test-automation-banking.vercel.app/');
  await page.getByPlaceholder('Enter Email').fill('leela@gmail.com');
  await page.getByPlaceholder('Enter Password').fill('Leela@123');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' }).click();
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);
});
```

Then:

```bash
npx playwright test week1/generated --list
```

**What you should see.** The file is **absent** from the listing. Not skipped, not failing — invisible. The runner never opened it, so a genuine test sat there proving nothing.

Rename it to `day4-practice-broken.spec.ts` and re-run `--list`: now it appears, and the count goes up by one. Delete the file when you are done.

**Why this is the day's real lesson.** Every other mistake in this course announces itself with a red failure. This one announces nothing at all — the suite stays green *because the test is not running*. `--list` is the cheap habit that catches it: it answers "what will actually run?" before you trust a green tick.
